            <div xml:id="d439">

                <pb facs="0815" n="695" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg695"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102Ic/30: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-04-29T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 29,
                                1922—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 22—10:30
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>179. My despatch number 45, September 29th, 1921.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d439-f1"
                    /> On April 26th diplomatic corps, in consequence of movement of Chinese troops
                    in the provinces, reminded Chinese Government of dean’s note August 30,
                        1921,<anchor n="31" xml:id="d439-f2"/> and reaffirmed the position that
                    foreign powers will insist that Chinese Government hold higher military
                    authorities personally and individually responsible for damage to foreign
                    interests, by action or failure to act, of their respective troops in territory
                    under their protection.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
